<!doctype linuxdoc system>

<article>
<title>Museum of Electronic Games & Art MEGA65-specific information for cc65
<author><url url="mailto:polluks+cc65@sdf.org" name="Stefan A. Haubenthal">

<abstract>
An overview over the MEGA65 run-time system as it's implemented for the cc65 C
compiler.
</abstract>

<!-- Table of contents -->
<toc>

<!-- Begin the document -->

<sect>Overview<p>

The MEGA65 is a modern 8-bit computer that is based on the never-released
Commodore 65. It uses the 45GS02 CPU and has a VIC-IV graphics controller
with 256 colors and an 80-column text mode. It has two CIA (6526) I/O chips
and SID sound chips. It supports file storage on Secure Digital cards, and
allows two joysticks and a mouse to be connected.

This file contains an overview of the MEGA65 run-time system as it comes with
the cc65 C compiler. It describes the memory layout, MEGA65-specific header
files, available drivers, and any pitfalls specific to that platform.

Please note that the functions mentioned here are described in detail in the
separate <url url="funcref.html" name="function reference">. Even functions
marked as "platform dependent" may be available on more than one platform.
Please see the function reference for more information.



<sect>Binary format<p>

The standard binary output format generated by the linker for the MEGA65 target
is a machine language program that's prepended with a 16-bit load address and a
one-line BASIC stub which selects bank 0 and calls the machine language part via
SYS. That means that a program can be loaded as a BASIC program, and started
with RUN. It is, of course, possible to change that behaviour by using a
modified program-header file and linker config.



<sect>Memory layout<p>

cc65-generated programs with the default setup use the memory configuration
that is active when the program is started. The usable memory range is
&dollar;2001 - &dollar;7FFF. The area above &dollar;7FFF is not used by the
default configuration, because it may contain ROM, I/O, or memory that is
reserved by the MEGA65 firmware.

Special locations:

<descrip>
  <tag/Stack/
  The C run-time stack is located at &dollar;8000, and grows downward.

  <tag/Heap/
  The C heap is located at the end of the program, and grows toward the C
  run-time stack.

  <tag/Zero page/
  The default configuration uses the zero page locations &dollar;18 -
  &dollar;31. The rest of page zero is reserved for BASIC and the Kernal.
</descrip><p>



<sect>Linker configurations<p>

The ld65 linker comes with a default config. file for the MEGA65, which
is used via <tt/-t mega65/. The MEGA65 package comes with additional secondary
linker config. files which are used via <tt/-t mega65 -C &lt;configfile&gt;/.

The default configuration uses 26 bytes in the zero page, at &dollar;18 -
&dollar;31. (The rest of page zero is reserved for BASIC and the Kernal.)
The assembly configuration makes the zero page from &dollar;0002 to
&dollar;00FF available to the program.


<sect1>Default config. file (<tt/mega65.cfg/)<p>

The default configuration is tailored to C programs. It supplies the load
address and a small BASIC stub that starts the compiled program using a SYS
command.


<sect1><tt/mega65-asm.cfg/<p>

This configuration is made for Assembly programmers who don't need a special
setup. The default start address is &dollar;2001. It can be changed with the
linker command-line option <tt/--start-addr/ or <tt/-S/. All standard segments,
with the exception of <tt/ZEROPAGE/, are written to the output file;
and, a two-byte load address is prepended.

To use that config. file, assemble with <tt/-t mega65/, and link with <tt/-C
mega65-asm.cfg/. The former will make sure that the correct character
translations are in effect, while the latter supplies the actual config.
When using <tt/cl65/, use both command-line options.

Sample command line for <tt/cl65/:
<tscreen><verb>
cl65 -o file.prg -t mega65 -C mega65-asm.cfg source.s
</verb></tscreen>

To generate code that loads to &dollar;4000:
<tscreen><verb>
cl65 -o file.prg -Wl -S,$4000 -t mega65 -C mega65-asm.cfg source.s
</verb></tscreen>

It also is possible to add a small BASIC header to the program, that uses SYS
to jump to the program entry point (which is the start of the code segment).
The advantage is that the program can be started using RUN.

To generate a program with a BASIC SYS header, use
<tscreen><verb>
cl65 -o file.prg -u __EXEHDR__ -t mega65 -C mega65-asm.cfg source.s
</verb></tscreen>

Please note that, in this case, a changed start address doesn't make sense,
because the program must be loaded to BASIC's start address.



<sect>Platform-specific header files<p>

Programs containing MEGA65-specific code may use the <tt/mega65.h/ or <tt/cbm.h/
header files. Using the latter may be an option when writing code for more than
one CBM-like platform, because it includes <tt/mega65.h/, and declares several
functions common to all CBM-like platforms.


<sect1>MEGA65-specific functions<p>

There are no functions that are specific to the MEGA65. See the <url
url="funcref.html" name="function reference"> for the functions that are
available on all CBM-like platforms.

<tt/cpeekcolor()/ works differently on the MEGA65 than it does on other
platforms. Each character has two colors: background and text (foreground).
<tt/cpeekcolor()/ returns both colors. The high nybble describes the
background color, the low nybble describes the text color. For example, if the
function is used on the default screen, then it returns &dollar;61, which means
white-on-blue.


<sect1>CBM-specific functions<p>

Some functions are available for all (or, at least most) of the Commodore-like
machines. See the <url url="funcref.html" name="function reference"> for
declarations and usage.

<itemize>
<item>cbm_close()
<item>cbm_closedir()
<item>cbm_k_basin()
<item>cbm_k_bsout()
<item>cbm_k_chkin()
<item>cbm_k_ckout()
<item>cbm_k_close()
<item>cbm_k_clrch()
<item>cbm_k_getin()
<item>cbm_k_load()
<item>cbm_k_open()
<item>cbm_k_readst()
<item>cbm_k_save()
<item>cbm_k_second()
<item>cbm_k_setlfs()
<item>cbm_k_setnam()
<item>cbm_k_tksa()
<item>cbm_load()
<item>cbm_open()
<item>cbm_opendir()
<item>cbm_read()
<item>cbm_readdir()
<item>cbm_save()
<item>cbm_write()
<item>get_tv()
</itemize>


<sect1>Hardware access<p>

The following pseudo variables declared in the <tt/mega65.h/ header file do
allow access to hardware located in the address space. Some variables are
structures, accessing the struct fields will access the chip registers.

<descrip>

  <tag><tt/VIC/</tag>
  The <tt/VIC/ structure allows access to the VIC-IV (the graphics
  controller). The structure is currently identical to the VIC-II register
  layout; see the <tt/_vic2.h/ header file located in the include directory
  for the declaration of the structure.

  <tag><tt/SID1, SID2/</tag>
  Access to the two SID (sound interface device) chips is available via the
  <tt/SID1/ and <tt/SID2/ variables. The structure behind these variables is
  explained in <tt/_sid.h/.

  <tag><tt/CIA1, CIA2/</tag>
  Access to the two CIA (complex interface adapter) chips is available via
  the <tt/CIA1/ and <tt/CIA2/ variables. The structure behind these variables
  is explained in <tt/_6526.h/.

  <tag><tt/COLOR_RAM/</tag>
  A pointer to the color RAM at &dollar;D800.
</descrip><p>



<sect>Loadable drivers<p>

The names in the parentheses denote the symbols to be used for static linking
of the drivers.


<sect1>Graphics drivers<p>

No graphics drivers are currently available for the MEGA65.


<sect1>Extended memory drivers<p>

No extended memory drivers are currently available for the MEGA65.


<sect1>Joystick drivers<p>

The default drivers, <tt/joy_stddrv (joy_static_stddrv)/,
point to <tt/m65-stdjoy.joy (mega65_stdjoy_joy)/.

<descrip>
  <tag><tt/m65-stdjoy.joy (mega65_stdjoy_joy)/</tag>
  Supports up to two standard joysticks connected to the joystick ports of
  the MEGA65.
</descrip><p>


<sect1>Mouse drivers<p>

No mouse drivers are currently available for the MEGA65.


<sect1>RS232 device drivers<p>

No serial drivers are currently available for the MEGA65.



<sect>Limitations<p>

The MEGA65's firmware and memory configuration can change at any time. Some
changes could make old programs fail to work.



<sect>Other hints<p>


<sect1>RUN/STOP and ESC keys<p>

The <tt/RUN//<tt/STOP/ key stops a running program. Holding <tt/Shift/ while
pressing it runs the first program on disk.

The <tt/ESC/ key is used to start escape sequences. Press and release it, and
then press the key that selects the desired function. See the MEGA65 user
guide for the available escape sequences.


<sect1>Passing arguments to the program<p>

Command-line arguments can be passed to <tt/main()/. Because that is not
supported directly by BASIC, the following syntax was chosen:
<tscreen><verb>
    RUN:REM ARG1 " ARG2 IS QUOTED" ARG3 "" ARG5
</verb></tscreen>

<enum>
<item>Arguments are separated by spaces.
<item>Arguments may be quoted.
<item>Leading and trailing spaces around an argument are ignored. Spaces within
      a quoted argument are allowed.
<item>The first argument passed to <tt/main()/ is the program name.
<item>A maximum number of 10 arguments (including the program name) are
      supported.
</enum>


<sect1>Program return code<p>

The program return code (low byte) is passed back to BASIC by the use of its
<tt/ST/ variable.


<sect1>Interrupts<p>

The run-time for the MEGA65 uses routines marked as <tt/.INTERRUPTOR/ for
interrupt handlers. Such routines must be written as simple machine language
subroutines, and will be called automatically by the interrupt handler code
if they are linked into a program. See the discussion of the <tt/.CONDES/
feature in the <url url="ca65.html" name="assembler manual">.



